        "new_string": "      \"bidirectionalCoupling\": \"The original bidirectional coupling between game-engine and world-ui (2+9 crossings) has been resolved to unidirectional flow. The 2 reverse crossings (engine → world-ui) were caused by play.tsx being classified in game-engine while importing MobileInventory/TileInventory from world-ui — resolved by the play.tsx zone override. TilePreview.tsx was reclassified from game-engine to world-ui because its only consumers (MobileInventory, TileInventory) are in world-ui; its dependency on engine/index.ts follows the correct DAG direction (world-ui → game-engine). The remaining crossings (world-ui → game-engine) represent the expected unidirectional data flow: UI components consuming engine types, constants, and hooks through the barrel.\",\n      \"worldUiCoupling\": \"world-ui reports coupling 0.58 from 9 imports targeting game-engine. This is structurally expected: world-ui is a leaf UI-composition zone whose sole purpose is to render engine state. The 9 imports break down as: 5 type/constant imports from the engine barrel (TileType, TILE_TYPE_DEFS, WorldData, LevelData, SVG_WIDTH/SVG_HEIGHT), 2 TilePreview component imports, and 2 hook imports (useWorldSession, useRainbowEasterEgg). All follow the correct DAG direction (UI → engine). The coupling score is below the warning threshold and the risk level is healthy. Reducing these imports would require either duplicating engine types in the UI layer or adding an unnecessary abstraction layer.\""
